{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448330-ideas-for-a-sabbat-game-set-in-1930-s-new-orleans","id":1448330,"title":"Ideas for a Sabbat game set in 1930's New Orleans","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-12T10:13:50-07:00","id":1448330,"title":"Ideas for a Sabbat game set in 1930's New Orleans","content":"I am creating a Sabbat game set in the late twenties/early thirties New Orleans. In my personal canon, New Orleans has been a Sabbat city for centuries. I would like you guys to help me brainstorm some story ideas for this game. Thank you in advance for your help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T12:12:38-07:00","id":1448346,"title":"","content":"You can go by events.<br /> <br /> 1935 - Dillard University is founded and provides education to a large number of black Americans. Control over the university could be a source of conflict between academicly-inclined clans, or between racist and egalitarian vampires.<br /> 1930s. Struggle to preserve the French Quarter. Aside from personal conflicts and desires, this could connect to a struggle between terriotrial/herd vampires who want stasis (Tzimisce, Ventrue), mortals, and vampires interested in gentrifying or developing the district.<br /> <br /> Crime. Bonnie and Clyde are active in the region in the time period, as well as a number of bank robbers. Lots of gang activity is happening.<br /> <br /> There is a house in New Orleans they call the Rising Sun. And it could be an actual house with some sort of mystery tied to it. Perhaps it's the focus of a Golconda cult, or a place to make deals with the devil, or some sort of extradimensional locale to explore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T12:20:48-07:00","id":1448347,"title":"","content":"I will probably have more to add later, but for the moment, I will recommend Gary Krist's nonfiction book Empire of Sin, about New Orleans's underworld of crime, vice, and political corruption between about 1890 to 1930, with a special focus on the red light district known as Storyville.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T12:57:33-07:00","id":1448352,"title":"","content":"I realize you're asking for story advice, but something about someone with &quot;Cajun&quot; in their name asking for help with New Orleans strikes me as amusing. But anyway, you can probably hit up <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3110_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3110\" data-vbnamecard=\"3110\">False Epiphany</a> as they have been running a game (at least, it's been linked in the bio) for a while. Also, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/221/New-Orleans-by-Night-WW2209\" target=\"_blank\"><i>New Orleans by Night</i></a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/2364/City-of-the-Damned-New-Orleans\" target=\"_blank\"><i>City of the Damned: New Orleans</i></a> overlap in actual historical content, but could provide some jumping-off points. Also, you could have a series of behind-the-scenes activities build up to the <a href=\"https://www.nola.com/news/politics/article_38f76c80-3c6a-5759-abd5-f8480262749f.html\" target=\"_blank\">Long/Walmsley confrontation</a> in '34, which might be a perfect parallel to the Camarilla attempting to take a Sabbat city.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T13:27:25-07:00","id":1448353,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448330-ideas-for-a-sabbat-game-set-in-1930-s-new-orleans?p=1448352#post1448352\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I realize you're asking for story advice, but something about someone with &quot;Cajun&quot; in their name asking for help with New Orleans strikes me as amusing.<br /> Cheers!</div> </div> </div> </div>Being Cajun helps remarkably little with this sort of thing. It's not like I get a +5 racial bonus to New Orleans fictional tropes. New Orleans isn't even a Cajun town. We mostly live in small towns to the west of it.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T13:38:04-07:00","id":1448355,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3598_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3598\" data-vbnamecard=\"3598\">CajunKhan</a> That's a shame; I got allocated an extra 10 freebies when I moved here <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T17:41:42-07:00","id":1448370,"title":"","content":"I will mention that several of Anne Rice's early novels were based in New Orleans. Interview with the Vampire and The Witching Hour being two that had a big influence on the development of the World of Darkness and Vampire. There's also her non-supernatural novel The Feast of All Saints, about the gens de couleur libres/free people of color in New Orleans before the Civil War.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-13T09:51:00-07:00","id":1448432,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://www.gothpoodle.com/nola/\" target=\"_blank\">This site</a> has a fan-made sourcebook for a Sabbat-held New Orleans/greater Louisiana. I'd steal the hell out of it if I was running a Sabbat game in the city, and have stolen a decent bit from it besides. (To any players in my game reading this post, please don't read that link!)<br /> <br /> <br /> As Kammerer notes, the 1930s are when the Quarter starts to blossom into the tourist mecca that it is today, concurrent with increased local efforts at historic preservation. The Quarter was an extremely high-crime and disreputable area during the 1890s. (This trend largely started in the aftermath of the Civil War with the city's economic decline.) During the 1920s, the Quarter was a bohemian area that drew the likes of William Faulkner. It was good hunting, but the inflow of tourists makes it an extremely lucrative piece of territory.*<br /> <br /> There's plenty you might do around the conflict between elders who value the area's history, the &quot;disreputable&quot; types who moved in after the Civil War, and the Kindred encouraging the area's economic development (potentially the same Kindred as the elders, but also potentially a distinct group, to highlight that elders are bad at coming up with new ideas). All of them have ample reason to dislike each other: the elders liked the area as it was and dislike the hooligans who helped ruin it. The hooligans dislike the efforts of newer Kindred to clean the area up (again, the Quarter was absurdly high-crime and disreputable during Reconstruction, it was basically a Sabbat playground). The elders might be of two minds over Kindred who are spearheading efforts at historic preservation while also trying to &quot;disneyfy&quot; it for tourists, even if that won't be anything on the same scale as it is now. These Kindred, of course, could well see the prior two groups of Kindred as obstacles to their own plans for the area. All of this could play out against the backdrop of the second Sabbat Civil War, which ends in 1933, and the Sabbat's larger themes of hypocrisy. Everyone in the sect is supposedly equal, but the elders still impose their will on younger vampires while giving lip service to the idea of equality.<br /> <br /> * Things still have a ways to go in the '30s, though. The Quarter will only REALLY become the city's most valuable piece of Kindred real estate during the '50s. You obviously don't have as much tourism during the '30s because everyone is poor, while travel is more inconvenient during World War II because of fuel rationing. It's after the war ends, America's love affair with the automobile begins, and the boom of the interstate highway system in the '50s that domestic travel really takes off.<br /> <br /> Another interesting idea is that revitalizing the Quarter requires working with mortals, which the Sabbat is supposed to be against. New Orleans is a stubbornly independent &quot;do things our way&quot; city in a lot of ways, though (fun related fact: the Bloods and Crips haven't managed to establish themselves in the city because local gangs fuck them up), and it's easy to imagine the native Kindred harboring similar sentiments... and getting into conflicts with the Sabbat leadership, especially after the Second Civil War ends.<br /> <br /> The Inquisition probably has a very bad relationship with the city. It's a cradle of alternative spirituality. Since belief in Vodoun has been on the decline since the end of slavery, but it is much stronger in the 1930s than it is today. (And, of course, should be stronger in the World of Darkness than the real world anyway.) You can't tell a story in New Orleans without at least touching on Vodoun, and it's easy to see the Inquisition being extremely suspicious about the loa and Cainites who not only consort with these beings, but freely open themselves to possession. Out-of-town inquisitors giving locals a hard time is only likely to harden local sentiments against the sect's meddlesome leadership. While the Inquisition probably doesn't go so far as to declare &quot;all Cainite mambos and houngans are heretics who shall be put to death,&quot; it's easy to see them keeping spies in the city and looking for any excuse (real or manufactured) to charge prominent Vodouisants with infernalism.<br /> <br /> Speaking of Vodoun and larger Sabbat... the Setites. The Serpents of the Light are supposed to join the sect in the '70s. During the 1930s, you could see a lot of future groundwork for that getting laid. New Orleans makes total sense as a meeting place between the Sabbat and any Setites thinking of splitting from the main clan.<br /> <br /> Don't forget Cuba, either. It was one of the city's biggest trading partners since colonial days: the Cuban Revolution and subsequent U.S. embargo was a significant economic blow to the city. Tourism between the U.S. and Cuba was huge during Prohibition because it was where you could legally get booze, and lots of those tourists passed through New Orleans. Tourism declined pretty sharply from 1930-33 when the Depression made everyone poor and Prohibition was repealed, but it's easy to imagine a few down-on-their-luck Kindred who'd tied their fortunes to Cuba in the '30s.<br /> <br /> It's not hard to imagine Delphine LaLaurie as a Tzimisce. The twisted shit she did to her slaves has been exaggerated, but in the World of Darkness, it pretty much has to be true. If you want something less on the nose, one of her immediate relatives could have been a fiend instead. I would probably give her a necromantic focus (or dealings with necromantically inclined Sabbat), because her crimes assuredly left behind some pretty furious ghosts.<br /> <br /> In 1919, the city's famous <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Opera_House\" target=\"_blank\">French Opera House</a> burned down. New Orleans went without an opera company of its own until the 1940s, which was a big big deal: opera was <i>the</i> place for the well-to-do to be seen for a while. It's not hard to imagine that happening as the result of some Kindred feud, likely involving a Toreador, and one party or the other seeking revenge, or even stymieing efforts to build a new opera house.<br /> <br /> If you want to deal with some genuine infernalism to justify the Sabbat Inquisition's fears, look no further than the <a href=\"http://www.hauntedneworleanstours.com/devilbaby/\" target=\"_blank\">Devil Baby of New Orleans</a>. It's like something right out of American Horror Story: he even has Delphine LaLaurie and Marie Laveau as his godmother and adoptive mother!<br /> <br /> Speaking of Marie Laveau, basically every mambo and houngan in the city during the '30s will claim to be a descendant of hers. Some might even be for real: she had something like a dozen kids.<br /> <br /> The <a href=\"https://ghostcitytours.com/new-orleans/haunted-places/sultans-palace/\" target=\"_blank\">sultan's palace</a> is another cool local legend that's definitely true in the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> Samuel &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sam_Zemurray\" target=\"_blank\">Sam the Banana Man</a>&quot; Zemurray was a resident of the city for a while. There's a bunch of stuff you can do there with the city's ties to Latin America.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axeman_of_New_Orleans\" target=\"_blank\">The Axeman</a> is another great (and actually real!) serial killer. That note he left the cops is just wild, and they never caught him. He could be any number of monsters in the WoD from a demon to a vampire.<br /> <br /> The Comte de St. Germain also <a href=\"https://www.ancient-origins.net/unexplained-phenomena/vampire-new-orleans-mysterious-case-jacque-and-comte-de-st-germain-009019\" target=\"_blank\">supposedly showed up</a> in the city once during the 1910s. Lot of things he could be in the WoD too.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also, one fun detail is that many vampires in the city may not speak English (or care to) in the 1930s. The city was a French colony for the first 100-odd years of its history, and then after the Americans came in with the Louisiana Purchase, the French-speaking Creoles remained a culturally and linguistically distinct population. They'd already experienced their home city being traded back and forth between several foreign powers, so the latest handover wasn't anything new to them. It wasn't until the Civil War when the occupying Union government made English compulsory in schools (and many Creole families lost their fortunes), among other things, that the city's Creole culture began to decline in earnest. But even as late as the 1920s, you could still find old women in the French Quarter who still spoke <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louisiana_French\" target=\"_blank\">Louisiana French</a> as their first language. <br /> <br /> It's not hard to imagine that linguistic gulf existing between English-preferring neonates and francophonic elders. I had this last a very long time in my setting, with French remaining the language of court in Elysium until the '60s, and not completely dying out in everynight conversation until Hurricane Katrina. I would still expect to see a ton of French in the '30s, but resentment building among English-speaking American neonates over the language's continued use. (This would be &quot;plantation French&quot; rather than metropolitan French, or rural Louisiana French, which still persists to this day.) The Sabbat may or may not be more forward-thinking than the Camarilla when it comes to adopting English.<br /> <br /> <br /> Sources-wise, the <i>Secrets of New Orleans</i> sourcebook for Call of Cthulhu is set in the 1920s and offers a wealth of information, adventure hooks, and characters you can convert to Masquerade with a little tweaking. I found its history section in particular to be significantly more informative than the one in Masquerade's or Requiem's sourcebooks. It also offers an absolute ton of book recommendations; way more than either of the Vampire books do.<br /> <br /> As No One of Consequence notes, <i>Empire of Sin</i> is great.<br /> <br /> I'll also throw out <i>The World That Made New Orleans: From Spanish Silver to Congo Square</i> for a look at the city's earlier history. A fair few of the more obscure historic figures work great as vampires/ghosts/whatever without being too cheesy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448330-ideas-for-a-sabbat-game-set-in-1930-s-new-orleans?p=1448352#post1448352\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I realize you're asking for story advice, but something about someone with &quot;Cajun&quot; in their name asking for help with New Orleans strikes me as amusing. But anyway, you can probably hit up <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3110_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3110\" data-vbnamecard=\"3110\">False Epiphany</a> as they have been running a game (at least, it's been linked in the bio) for a while.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. Game celebrated its 6th anniversary just last month. Feel free to steal whatever content you like from there. It's set in the 2010s, but there's plenty of content relevant to the '30s and earlier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":3110,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","user":"False Epiphany","date":". 05-13-2021, 09:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-13T15:27:39-07:00","id":1448473,"title":"","content":"More things I just thought of: Air travel becomes big; like, really big. &quot;<a href=\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/travel/roadwarriorvoices/2016/01/10/this-is-what-it-was-like-to-fly-in-the-1930s/83283086/\" target=\"_blank\">Just 6,000 Americans traveled commercially by airplane in all of 1930, according to Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum . Only four years later, that number </a><a href=\"https://airandspace.si.edu/exhibitions/america-by-air/online/innovation/innovation15.cfm\" target=\"_blank\">would multiply by 75 times</a><a href=\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/travel/roadwarriorvoices/2016/01/10/this-is-what-it-was-like-to-fly-in-the-1930s/83283086/\" target=\"_blank\"> \u2014 450,000 passengers flew in 1934, a number that would be dwarfed once more in another four years, when 1.2 Million Americans traveled by air.</a>&quot; In 1929, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakefront_Airport\" target=\"_blank\">Shushan Airport</a> begins construction, and is completed by 1934 (and is a huge flex when you have your initials carved on every door knob, every light fixture and every counter top). Rail travel is declining in the '30s, as autos are becoming more affordable, but they'll attempt to bounce back in the latter half of the decade. Finally, water travel is big in the area, not just commercial. The bridges aren't around in the '30s, so the only way to the Westbank and the Northshore are ferries.<br /> <br /> Orleans Parish is pretty much defined by this point, but not nearly as highly developed. There's almost no Lakefront to speak of, though <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_End,_New_Orleans\" target=\"_blank\">West End</a> is a resort area with a boardwalk/amusement park. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontchartrain_Beach\" target=\"_blank\">The park</a> would be relocated in the early 1930's. <a href=\"https://www.nola.com/news/article_00cf8076-c916-11e9-be3a-fb784c9db541.html\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a> is a good, if sparse, map. There are a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Orleans_metropolitan_area\" target=\"_blank\">ridiculous amount</a> of tiny little towns surrounding New Orleans at this point.<br /> <br /> Finally, the <a href=\"https://livingnewdeal.org/us/la/new-orleans/\" target=\"_blank\">New Deal</a> is going to affect the area in a huge way in the late 1930's, renovating, restoring, and building all-new structures.<br /> <br /> I'm sure I'll think of more later.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-13T17:10:42-07:00","id":1448477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>False Epiphany</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448330-ideas-for-a-sabbat-game-set-in-1930-s-new-orleans?p=1448432#post1448432\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll also throw out <i>The World That Made New Orleans: From Spanish Silver to Congo Square</i> for a look at the city's earlier history. A fair few of the more obscure historic figures work great as vampires/ghosts/whatever without being too cheesy.</div> </div> </div> </div>I knew there was another book that I'd wanted to recommend but was blanking on it. Thank you. <br /> <br /> There's also an interesting oddity called New Orleans Vampires: History and Legend, which includes a number of interesting bits of folklore and urban legends from the city that can be added to a Vampire setting for flavor. For example, the Carter brothers, who were arrested in 1932 for holding multiple women prisoner in their home in order to feed on their blood. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-14T15:38:43-07:00","id":1448543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7jUii4gFGZQ\" data-code=\"7jUii4gFGZQ\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/7jUii4gFGZQ/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}